# Kite's grammar

This file is Kite's parser: the grammar of [`SPEC.md`](../SPEC.md), written as Avin values with the
parsing library. It is a literate Avin file: everything indented four columns is the program, and
everything at the margin explains it.

    module kite

    import dev.avinlang.parsing.{Input, ParseResult, Parser, Position, RegexParsers, acceptMatch, acceptSeq, chainl1, commit, elem, err, failure, guard, ident, literal, not, opt, rep, rep1, rep1sep, success}

The parser reads a whole file and answers a `Module`, or the first syntax error with its line and
column. Expressions, statements and types can be parsed on their own too, which is how the tests
look at one rule at a time.

## White space belongs to the grammar

Kite's white space is spaces, tabs, line ends, `// line comments` and `/* block comments */`, which
nest. It is skipped before every token, line ends included, so a token never has to say anything
about the space before it. A grammar says what its white space is by being a `RegexParsers`:

    // The Kite grammar's white space: every white space character, line comments, and block comments,
    // which nest.
    object Kite: RegexParsers
        override whiteSpace() -> Parser[unit] = space

The comment parsers read characters, never tokens, so that they can run inside the white space
parser without skipping white space themselves.

A block comment that is never closed is the one mistake white space can hold, and white space
reports it: where a `/*` does not begin a whole comment, `err` fails past a commit point, which the
library takes as an error of the text, reported where the `/*` stands. The token about to be read
fails with it, so the error is found wherever white space is skipped: before a statement, inside an
expression, at the end of the file.

    // `//` to the end of the line, the line end not included.
    lazy val lineComment: Parser[unit] = acceptSeq("//".chars()) ~ rep(elem("comment", c -> c != '\n')) ^^^ ()

    // `/*` to the matching `*/`, other block comments nested inside.
    lazy val blockComment: Parser[unit] = acceptSeq("/*".chars()) ~ rep(blockComment | not(acceptSeq("*/".chars())) ~> elem("comment", c -> true) ^^^ ()) ~ acceptSeq("*/".chars()) ^^^ ()

    // A `/*` that begins no whole comment: an error where it stands.
    lazy val openComment: Parser[unit] = guard(acceptSeq("/*".chars())) ~> err[unit](commentMessage)

    val commentMessage = "`*/` to close the comment that begins here"

    // Everything skipped before a token.
    lazy val space: Parser[unit] = rep(elem("space", c -> c.isWhitespace()) ^^^ () | lineComment | blockComment | openComment) ^^^ ()

## Line ends

Since every token skips line ends, a line end is never in the way; the grammar asks about one only
where it matters. A newline ends a statement, so a statement has to be followed by a line end, by
the `}` closing its block, or by the end of the text. And an expression outside brackets must not
continue onto the next line: an operator, a call's `(`, an index's `[` or a field's `.` after a line
end begins a new statement.

Both are questions about the white space between the last token and the next, and the input
answers them: skip the grammar's white space as a token would, look at what was skipped for a line
end, and go back. A line end anywhere in it counts, inside a block comment too. Skipping white space
reports nothing, so neither question adds to an error's message unless it says so; white space that
holds an unclosed comment has reported that, and the question fails with it.

    // Whether the white space before the next token holds a line end, in a comment or not. Reads
    // nothing.
    lineBreakAhead(input: Input) -> bool
        val start = input.offset
        input.handleWhiteSpace()
        val end = input.offset
        input.offset = start
        var i = start
        while i < end
            if int(input.source.byteAt(i)) == 0x0a then return true
            i += 1
        false

    // Succeeds, reading nothing, where the next token is on the same line.
    lazy val sameLine: Parser[unit] = Parser(input -> if lineBreakAhead(input) then None else Some(()))

    // Succeeds, reading nothing, where the line ends, or the text does, before the next token; fails
    // at that token, expecting the end of the line.
    lazy val lineEnd: Parser[unit] = Parser(input ->
        if lineBreakAhead(input) then return Some(())
        val start = input.offset
        if !input.handleWhiteSpace() then return None
        val atEnd = input.atEnd()
        if !atEnd then input.expect("end of line")
        input.offset = start
        if atEnd then Some(()) else None)

    // What may follow a statement: the end of its line, or the `}` that closes its block.
    lazy val stmtEnd: Parser[unit] = lineEnd | guard(literal("}")) ^^^ ()

Inside `(`, `[`, a record type's `{` and an interpolation's `{` the newline rule is off: an
operator may begin a line there, since no statement can end inside a bracket. The expression rules
are therefore made twice, once for each **mode**, and differ only in `gap`, the parser run before an
operator or a suffix: `sameLine` in a statement, nothing at all inside a bracket. A bracket switches
to `nested`, and a block, whose `{` holds statements, switches back to `lined`.

    // The expression rules where a line end ends the expression: in statements.
    lazy val lined: Exprs = Exprs(false)

    // The expression rules inside brackets, where line ends are white space like any other.
    lazy val nested: Exprs = Exprs(true)

## Positions

A node carries the line and column it begins at. `here` skips white space and answers the position
of the token that follows; `point` answers the position without skipping, for the inside of a string.
Each is a parser written as a function of the input, as the library's own leaves are.

    // The position of the next token, after white space.
    lazy val here: Parser[Position] = Parser(input ->
        if !input.handleWhiteSpace() then return None
        Some(input.pos()))

    // The position of the next character, nothing skipped.
    lazy val point: Parser[Position] = Parser(input -> Some(input.pos()))

    // White space, skipped.
    lazy val skipSpace: Parser[unit] = Parser(input ->
        if !input.handleWhiteSpace() then return None
        Some(()))

## Brackets, and the error that names the opening one

A bracket that is never closed is the commonest syntax error, and the place it is noticed can be
far from the place it was made. So a closing bracket that is missing is reported against the opening
one: *expected `)` to close `(` at 3:10*. The opening bracket's position is read first and the rest
of the rule is made from it with `flatMap`. The closing bracket is a commit point: once a bracket is
open, nothing but its contents and its closing bracket can follow, and no other alternative is
worth trying.

`withFailureMessage` renames the closing bracket's failure only when the parse got no further than
the closing bracket. When something inside the brackets went wrong further on, that is the error,
and it is reported as it is.

    // `inner` between `open` and `close`, with the position of `open`.
    enclosed[T](open: string, inner: -> Parser[T], close: string) -> Parser[(Position, T)] =
        (here <~ literal(open)).flatMap(at -> inner <~ closing(open, close, at) ^^ (v -> (at, v)))

    // The `close` that matches the `open` at `at`, which must be there.
    closing(open: string, close: string, at: Position) -> Parser[string] =
        commit(literal(close).withFailureMessage(s"`$close` to close `$open` at $at"))

A list in brackets separates its items with commas and may end with one, so that a list written one
item to a line can end every line with a comma.

    // `p`s separated by commas, none included, with an optional comma after the last.
    commaList[T](p: -> Parser[T]) -> Parser[List[T]] =
        opt(p ~ rep(literal(",") ~> p) <~ opt(literal(","))) ^^ (o -> listed(o))

    // The list a `commaList` read: the first item and the rest, or nothing.
    listed[T](o: Option[(T, List[T])]) -> List[T] = o match
        Some((first, rest)) -> first :: rest
        None -> []

## Words

The keywords are the words a name may not be. A keyword is its letters not followed by another
letter, digit or `_`, so `iffy` and `format` are names. What `not` looks for is not reported when the
keyword fails, so `if)` says only what the grammar wanted after `if`; where `not` refuses the letter
after the keyword's own, the library reports the keyword as expected where it began, as though the
text held no keyword there at all.

    // The words that are not names.
    val keywords = ["module", "import", "fn", "type", "let", "var", "if", "else", "while", "for", "in", "match", "return", "break", "continue", "true", "false"]

    // Whether `c` may continue a name.
    nameChar(c: char) -> bool = c == '_' || c.isLetterOrDigit()

    // The keyword `word`.
    kw(word: string) -> Parser[string] = literal(word) <~ not(elem("letter", nameChar))

A name is the library's `ident`, a letter in any script or `_` then letters, digits and `_`, that is
not a keyword. A keyword where a name belongs is reported where it stands, naming it.

    // A name: an identifier that is not a keyword.
    lazy val name: Parser[string] = Parser(input ->
        if !input.handleWhiteSpace() then return None
        val start = input.offset
        anyName.run(input) match
            Some(n) if keywords.contains(n) ->
                input.offset = start
                input.fail(s"name (`$n` is a keyword)")
            r -> r)

    // An identifier, keywords included, expected as a `name`.
    lazy val anyName: Parser[string] = ident.withFailureMessage("name")

    // Names joined by `.`: `geometry.shapes`.
    lazy val dottedName: Parser[string] = rep1sep(name, sameLine ~> literal(".")) ^^ (parts -> parts.mkString("."))

## Numbers

A number is digits, then optionally a `.` and digits, then optionally an exponent; it is a float
when it has either. A `.` not followed by a digit is not part of the number, so `0..10` is a range
and `1.max` a field. A number running into a letter, `12abc`, is an error at the letter. An integer
too large for `i64` is an error at the number, and a final one: no other rule would read it better.
The number is read by a parser written as a function of the input, because its pieces are bytes
and no piece is worth reporting on its own.

    // The byte at `at` as a number, or -1 past the end of `text`.
    byteOf(text: string, at: int) -> int = if at < text.byteLength() then int(text.byteAt(at)) else -1

    // Whether the byte at `at` is an ASCII digit.
    digitAt(text: string, at: int) -> bool = byteOf(text, at) >= 0x30 && byteOf(text, at) <= 0x39

    // Moves past ASCII digits, answering how many.
    skipDigits(text: string, from: int) -> int
        var i = from
        while digitAt(text, i)
            i += 1
        i - from

    // An integer or a float literal. An integer too large is the whole error: whatever else could have
    // stood where it does is not mentioned.
    lazy val number: Parser[Expr] = numeral.withErrorMessage(tooLarge)

    val tooLarge = "integer of at most 9223372036854775807, the largest i64"

    // An integer or a float literal, failing past a commit point when an integer is too large.
    lazy val numeral: Parser[Expr] = Parser(input ->
        if !input.handleWhiteSpace() then return None
        val text = input.source
        val start = input.offset
        val at = input.pos()
        val whole = skipDigits(text, start)
        if whole == 0 then return input.fail("number")
        input.offset += whole
        var isFloat = false
        if byteOf(text, input.offset) == 0x2e && digitAt(text, input.offset + 1)
            input.offset += 1
            input.offset += skipDigits(text, input.offset)
            isFloat = true
        if byteOf(text, input.offset) == 0x65 || byteOf(text, input.offset) == 0x45
            var i = input.offset + 1
            if byteOf(text, i) == 0x2b || byteOf(text, i) == 0x2d then i += 1
            val digits = skipDigits(text, i)
            if digits > 0
                input.offset = i + digits
                isFloat = true
        if !input.atEnd() && nameChar(input.first()) then return input.fail("digit")
        val written = text.slice(start, input.offset)
        if isFloat then return Some(FloatLit(written.toRealOption().getOrElse(0.0), at))
        written.toLongOption() match
            Some(v) -> Some(IntLit(v, at))
            None ->
                input.offset = start
                input.committed = true
                input.fail(tooLarge))

## Strings, and switching lexical modes

Inside a string the rules of tokens are off: white space is text, `//` is text, and `{` begins an
interpolated expression. The string is read with character parsers, which skip nothing, so the
switch into the string's mode is simply using them. The switch back, inside `{…}`, is using the
token parsers again: `nested.expr` skips white space before each token as every expression does,
and may itself hold a string, which holds an interpolation, to any depth.

    // Text of a string: any characters but `"`, `\`, `{` and a line end.
    lazy val textChunk: Parser[StrPart] = rep1(elem("string character", c -> c != '"' && c != '\\' && c != '{' && c != '\n')) ^^ (cs -> Text(cs.mkString("")))

An escape is `\` and one character, or `\u{…}` with one to six hex digits naming a Unicode scalar
value. Anything else after a `\` is an error at that character, and final.

    // The text an escape stands for, after its `\`.
    lazy val escapeBody: Parser[string] = (acceptMatch("escape", escapes) | unicodeEscape).withFailureMessage("escape (`\\n`, `\\t`, `\\r`, `\\0`, `\\\\`, `\\\"`, `\\{`, `\\}` or `\\u{…}`)")

    // The one-character escapes.
    val escapes: PartialFunction[char, string] = match
        'n' -> "\n"
        't' -> "\t"
        'r' -> "\r"
        '0' -> "\u{0}"
        '\\' -> "\\"
        '"' -> "\""
        '{' -> "{"
        '}' -> "}"

    // `u{…}`: the character with that code.
    lazy val unicodeEscape: Parser[string] = (elem('u') ~> commit(elem('{') ~> rep1(hexDigit) <~ elem('}')).withErrorMessage("`{`, hex digits and `}` after `\\u`")).flatMap(ds -> scalar(ds))

    // A hex digit's value.
    lazy val hexDigit: Parser[int] = acceptMatch("hex digit", hexValue)

    val hexValue: PartialFunction[char, int] = match
        c if c >= '0' && c <= '9' -> int(c) - 48
        c if c >= 'a' && c <= 'f' -> int(c) - 87
        c if c >= 'A' && c <= 'F' -> int(c) - 55

    // The character the hex digits `ds` name, which must be a Unicode scalar value.
    scalar(ds: List[int]) -> Parser[string]
        var code = 0
        for d in ds
            if code > 0x10ffff then break
            code = code * 16 + d
        if ds.length() > 6 || code > 0x10ffff || (code >= 0xd800 && code <= 0xdfff) then err[string]("Unicode scalar value (at most 10FFFF, and not D800 to DFFF) in `\\u{…}`")
        else success(s"${char(code)}")

    // `\` and an escape.
    lazy val escape: Parser[StrPart] = elem('\\') ~> commit(escapeBody) ^^ (t -> Text(t))

    // `{`, an expression, `}`.
    lazy val interpolation: Parser[StrPart] = (point <~ elem('{')).flatMap(at -> commit(nested.expr) <~ closing("{", "}", at)) ^^ (e -> Interp(e))

A string that is not closed before its line ends is reported against its opening quote. The
closing quote is a commit point, and its message replaces whatever else the string's pieces expected
where they stopped.

    // A string whose pieces are `part`s, with the position of its opening quote.
    quotedWith(part: -> Parser[StrPart]) -> Parser[(Position, List[StrPart])] =
        (here <~ elem('"')).flatMap(at -> rep(part) <~ commit(elem('"')).withErrorMessage(s"`\"` to close the string at $at") ^^ (parts -> (at, merged(parts))))

    // The pieces of a string with neighbouring text joined, so `"a\tb"` is one piece of text.
    merged(parts: List[StrPart]) -> List[StrPart]
        val out = Buffer[StrPart]()
        var text: Option[StringWriter] = None
        for p in parts
            p match
                Text(t) -> text match
                    Some(w) -> w.write(t)
                    None ->
                        val w = StringWriter()
                        w.write(t)
                        text = Some(w)
                Interp(_) ->
                    text match
                        Some(w) -> out.addOne(Text(w.text()))
                        None -> ()
                    text = None
                    out.addOne(p)
        text match
            Some(w) -> out.addOne(Text(w.text()))
            None -> ()
        out.toList()

    // A string literal, with interpolation.
    lazy val stringLit: Parser[Expr] = quotedWith(textChunk | escape | interpolation) ^^ (match (at, parts) -> StrLit(parts, at))

A pattern's string is matched against, so it cannot interpolate; a `{` in one is an error that says
how to write a brace.

    // A `{` inside a pattern's string.
    lazy val braceInPattern: Parser[StrPart] = (guard(elem('{')) ~> err[StrPart]("text: a pattern's string cannot interpolate, so write a brace as `\\{`")).withErrorMessage("text: a pattern's string cannot interpolate, so write a brace as `\\{`")

    // A string without interpolation.
    lazy val plainString: Parser[(Position, string)] = quotedWith(textChunk | escape | braceInPattern) ^^ (match (at, parts) -> (at, parts.map(textOf).mkString("")))

    // The text of a piece of a string; an interpolation has none.
    textOf(p: StrPart) -> string = p match
        Text(t) -> t
        Interp(_) -> ""

## Types

A type is a name, `i64` and `Shape` alike, or a fixed array `[T; n]`. Which names are types is the
checker's question.

    // A type.
    lazy val typeRef: Parser[TypeRef] = (here ~ name ^^ (match (at, n) -> NamedType(n, at)) | arrayType).withFailureMessage("type")

    // `[T; n]`.
    lazy val arrayType: Parser[TypeRef] = enclosed("[", typeRef ~ (literal(";") ~> arraySize), "]") ^^ (match (at, (t, n)) -> ArrayType(t, n, at))

    // The length of an array type: a whole number.
    lazy val arraySize: Parser[long] = number.^?(match IntLit(n, _) -> n, e -> "whole number for the array's length")

## Expressions

The precedence levels, loosest first, each a rule of the next: `||`; `&&`; the comparisons, which do
not chain; the ranges, which do not chain either; `+` and `-`; `*`, `/` and `%`; the prefix `-` and
`!`; the suffixes, a call, an index and a field; and the primaries. Each level's operator is read
after `gap`, which is where the newline rule lives.

An operator that is the beginning of a longer one is not that operator: `+` is not the `+` of `+=`,
and `.` is not the `.` of `..`. `not` says so.

    // `text` as an operator, where the character after it is none of `notBefore`.
    opTok(text: string, notBefore: string) -> Parser[string] = literal(text) <~ not(elem("operator character", c -> notBefore.contains(s"$c")))

    lazy val compareOp: Parser[string] = literal("==") | literal("!=") | literal("<=") | literal(">=") | literal("<") | literal(">")
    lazy val rangeOp: Parser[string] = literal("..=") | literal("..")
    lazy val addOp: Parser[string] = opTok("+", "=") | opTok("-", "=")
    lazy val mulOp: Parser[string] = opTok("*", "=") | opTok("/", "=") | opTok("%", "=")
    lazy val unaryOp: Parser[string] = opTok("-", "=") | opTok("!", "=")
    lazy val assignOp: Parser[string] = (literal("+=") | literal("-=") | literal("*=") | literal("/=") | literal("%=") | opTok("=", "=>")).withFailureMessage("operator")

    // A binary node or a range: what each operator makes of its operands and its position.
    makeBinary(op: string, l: Expr, r: Expr, at: Position) -> Expr = op match
        ".." -> RangeExpr(l, r, false, at)
        "..=" -> RangeExpr(l, r, true, at)
        _ -> Binary(op, l, r, at)

    // An operand and, when one was read, an operator and a second operand: their node.
    joined(e: (Expr, Option[((Expr, Expr) -> Expr, Expr)])) -> Expr = e match
        (l, Some((f, r))) -> f(l, r)
        (l, None) -> l

The rules of one mode. `chainl1` folds each left-grouping level as it reads. The comparisons and
the ranges read at most one operator, and a second one is an error at the second operator, past a
commit point: `a < b < c` asks for `&&`.

    // The expression rules of one mode: `nested` inside brackets, where a line end is white space, and
    // not in statements, where it ends the expression.
    class Exprs(val inBrackets: bool)
        // What comes before an operator or a suffix.
        lazy val gap: Parser[unit] = if inBrackets then success(()) else sameLine

        // An expression.
        lazy val expr: Parser[Expr] = chainl1(conjunction, binary(literal("||")))

        lazy val conjunction: Parser[Expr] = chainl1(comparison, binary(literal("&&")))

        lazy val comparison: Parser[Expr] = rangeExpr ~ opt(binary(compareOp) ~ rangeExpr) <~ unchained(compareOp, "`&&` between two comparisons, which do not chain") ^^ joined

        lazy val rangeExpr: Parser[Expr] = additive ~ opt(binary(rangeOp) ~ additive) <~ unchained(rangeOp, "the end of the range, since ranges do not chain") ^^ joined

        lazy val additive: Parser[Expr] = chainl1(multiplicative, binary(addOp))

        lazy val multiplicative: Parser[Expr] = chainl1(prefixed, binary(mulOp))

        lazy val prefixed: Parser[Expr] = (here ~ unaryOp ~ prefixed ^^ (match ((at, op), x) -> Unary(op, x, at))
            | suffixed).withFailureMessage("expression")

        lazy val suffixed: Parser[Expr] = primary ~ rep(gap ~> suffix) ^^ (match (e, fs) -> fs.foldLeft(e, (acc, f) -> f(acc)))

        // A call's arguments, an index or a field: what it makes of the expression before it.
        lazy val suffix: Parser[Expr -> Expr] = (enclosed("(", commaList(argument), ")") ^^ (match (at, args) -> (f: Expr) -> Call(f, args, at))
            | enclosed("[", nested.expr, "]") ^^ (match (at, i) -> (t: Expr) -> Index(t, i, at))
            | here ~ (opTok(".", ".") ~> name) ^^ (match (at, n) -> (t: Expr) -> Field(t, n, at))).withFailureMessage("operator")

        lazy val primary: Parser[Expr] = (number
            | stringLit
            | here ~ (kw("true") ^^^ true | kw("false") ^^^ false) ^^ (match (at, b) -> BoolLit(b, at))
            | ifExpr
            | matchExpr
            | block
            | arrayLit
            | enclosed("(", nested.expr, ")") ^^ (match (_, e) -> e)
            | here ~ name ^^ (match (at, n) -> Name(n, at))).withFailureMessage("expression")

`if` and `match` read their condition and their value in the mode they are in, and their blocks
and arms as statements are read. `else` may begin a line: no statement begins with it, so there is
nothing for the line end to have ended.

        lazy val ifExpr: Parser[Expr] = here ~ (kw("if") ~! (expr ~ block ~ opt(kw("else") ~! (ifExpr | block)))) ^^ (match (at, (_, ((c, t), o))) -> If(c, t, o.map(match (_, e) -> e), at))

        lazy val matchExpr: Parser[Expr] = here ~ (kw("match") ~! (expr ~ enclosed("{", arms, "}"))) ^^ (match (at, (_, (x, (_, arms)))) -> Match(x, arms, at))

        // An operator of this mode: read after `gap`, making its node from its operands.
        binary(op: Parser[string]) -> Parser[(Expr, Expr) -> Expr] = (gap ~> here ~ op ^^ (match (at, o) -> (l: Expr, r: Expr) -> makeBinary(o, l, r, at))).withFailureMessage("operator")

        // Nothing, where `op` does not follow; where it does, an error at it expecting `what`.
        unchained(op: Parser[string], what: string) -> Parser[unit] = not(gap ~> op) | (skipSpace ~> err[unit](what)).withErrorMessage(what)

A call's argument may be named, `P(x: 1.0, y: 2.0)`, which is how a record or a variant is built.
The name is read only where a name and `:` are both there, which `not(not(…))` sees without
recording anything, so a message about an argument never offers a `:` after an expression that
happens to be a name.

    // An argument: an expression, perhaps after a name and `:`.
    lazy val argument: Parser[Arg] = opt(not(not(name ~ literal(":"))) ~> name <~ literal(":")) ~ nested.expr ^^ (match (n, v) -> Arg(n, v))

An array is its items in brackets, or one item, `;` and a count: `[0.0; 8]`.

    lazy val arrayLit: Parser[Expr] = enclosed("[", opt(nested.expr ~ arrayRest), "]") ^^ (match
        (at, Some((first, rest))) -> rest(first, at)
        (at, None) -> ArrayLit([], at))

    // What follows an array's first item: `;` and a count, or more items.
    lazy val arrayRest: Parser[(Expr, Position) -> Expr] = literal(";") ~> nested.expr ^^ (c -> (first: Expr, at: Position) -> ArrayRepeat(first, c, at))
        | rep(literal(",") ~> nested.expr) <~ opt(literal(",")) ^^ (rest -> (first: Expr, at: Position) -> ArrayLit(first :: rest, at))

## Statements and blocks

A block is statements in braces, each ended by its line or by the `}`. Its value is its last
statement's, when that is an expression, which is the checker's business; the parser keeps the
statements in order.

    // `{`, statements, `}`.
    lazy val block: Parser[Expr] = enclosed("{", rep(stmt <~ stmtEnd), "}") ^^ (match (at, stmts) -> BlockExpr(stmts, at))

    // A statement.
    lazy val stmt: Parser[Stmt] = (letStmt
        | whileStmt
        | forStmt
        | returnStmt
        | here <~ kw("break") ^^ (at -> Break(at))
        | here <~ kw("continue") ^^ (at -> Continue(at))
        | exprStmt
        | leadingOperator).withFailureMessage("statement")

    lazy val letStmt: Parser[Stmt] = here ~ (kw("let") ^^^ false | kw("var") ^^^ true) ~! (name ~ opt(literal(":") ~> typeRef) ~ (opTok("=", "=") ~> lined.expr)) ^^ (match ((at, mutable), ((n, t), v)) -> Let(mutable, n, t, v, at))

    lazy val whileStmt: Parser[Stmt] = here ~ (kw("while") ~! (lined.expr ~ block)) ^^ (match (at, (_, (c, b))) -> While(c, b, at))

    lazy val forStmt: Parser[Stmt] = here ~ (kw("for") ~! (name ~ (kw("in") ~> lined.expr) ~ block)) ^^ (match (at, (_, ((n, over), b))) -> For(n, over, b, at))

A `return`'s value is on its line: `return` at the end of a line returns nothing, and the line
after it is the next statement.

    lazy val returnStmt: Parser[Stmt] = here ~ (kw("return") ~> opt(sameLine ~> lined.expr)) ^^ (match (at, v) -> Return(v, at))

An expression statement may be the target of an assignment. Whether the target is a place, a
name, an element or a field, is the checker's question; the parser reads any expression there.

    lazy val exprStmt: Parser[Stmt] = lined.expr ~ opt(sameLine ~> here ~ assignOp ~ lined.expr) ^^ (match
        (e, None) -> ExprStmt(e)
        (target, Some(((at, op), v))) -> Assign(op, target, v, at))

One mistake is common enough to be recognised. An operator that begins a line does not continue
the line before it, so a statement cannot begin with one, and the error says where to move it.

    // The operators that cannot begin an expression.
    lazy val binaryOperator: Parser[string] = literal("||") | literal("&&") | literal("==") | literal("!=") | literal("<") | literal(">") | literal("+") | literal("*") | literal("/") | literal("%") | literal("..") | literal(".") | literal("=")

    // An operator at the start of a line, as an error.
    lazy val leadingOperator: Parser[Stmt] = (skipSpace ~> guard(binaryOperator) ~> err[Stmt](leadingMessage)).withErrorMessage(leadingMessage)

    val leadingMessage = "statement: an operator after a line end does not continue the line above, so end that line with it"

## Patterns and arms

A pattern is `_`, a literal, a name, or a variant's name with patterns for its fields. A name alone
is a binding or a variant without fields; the checker tells which.

    // A pattern.
    lazy val pattern: Parser[Pattern] = (here <~ (literal("_") <~ not(elem("letter", nameChar))) ^^ (at -> WildPat(at))
        | here ~ opt(literal("-")) ~ number ^^ (match ((at, minus), n) -> numberPattern(minus is Some(_), n, at))
        | plainString ^^ (match (at, t) -> StrPat(t, at))
        | here ~ (kw("true") ^^^ true | kw("false") ^^^ false) ^^ (match (at, b) -> BoolPat(b, at))
        | namedPattern).withFailureMessage("pattern")

    // A name, perhaps with patterns for a variant's fields.
    lazy val namedPattern: Parser[Pattern] = here ~ name ~ opt(enclosed("(", commaList(pattern), ")")) ^^ (match
        ((at, n), Some((_, fields))) -> VariantPat(n, fields, at)
        ((at, n), None) -> NamePat(n, at))

    // A number pattern, negated after a `-`.
    numberPattern(minus: bool, n: Expr, at: Position) -> Pattern = n match
        IntLit(v, _) -> IntPat(if minus then -v else v, at)
        FloatLit(v, _) -> FloatPat(if minus then -v else v, at)
        _ -> WildPat(at)

An arm is a pattern, `=>` and an expression, ended by its line, a `,`, or the `}` of the `match`.
`=>` may end a line, as any operator may.

    // A `match` arm.
    lazy val arm: Parser[Arm] = pattern ~! (literal("=>") ~> lined.expr) ^^ (match (p, body) -> Arm(p, body))

    // A `match`'s arms.
    lazy val arms: Parser[List[Arm]] = rep(arm <~ (literal(",") ^^^ () | stmtEnd))

## Declarations

A function has typed parameters, an optional result type after `->`, and a block. A type is an
alias of another, a record of named fields in braces, or a sum of variants, each after a `|`, which
may begin a line: the declaration is still going.

    // A function.
    lazy val fnDecl: Parser[Item] = here ~ (kw("fn") ~! (name ~ enclosed("(", commaList(param), ")") ~ opt(literal("->") ~> typeRef) ~ block)) ^^ (match (at, (_, (((n, (_, ps)), r), body))) -> FnDecl(n, ps, r, body, at))

    lazy val param: Parser[Param] = here ~ name ~ (literal(":") ~> typeRef) ^^ (match ((at, n), t) -> Param(n, t, at))

    // A type declaration.
    lazy val typeDecl: Parser[Item] = here ~ (kw("type") ~! (name ~ (opTok("=", "=") ~> typeDef))) ^^ (match (at, (_, (n, d))) -> TypeDecl(n, d, at))

    lazy val typeDef: Parser[TypeDef] = rep1(literal("|") ~> variant) ^^ (vs -> Sum(vs))
        | enclosed("{", commaList(fieldDecl), "}") ^^ (match (_, fs) -> Record(fs))
        | typeRef ^^ (t -> Alias(t))

    lazy val variant: Parser[Variant] = here ~ name ~ opt(enclosed("(", commaList(fieldDecl), ")")) ^^ (match
        ((at, n), Some((_, fs))) -> Variant(n, fs, at)
        ((at, n), None) -> Variant(n, [], at))

    lazy val fieldDecl: Parser[FieldDecl] = here ~ name ~ (literal(":") ~> typeRef) ^^ (match ((at, n), t) -> FieldDecl(n, t, at))

    // A declaration.
    lazy val item: Parser[Item] = fnDecl | typeDecl

## A file

A file is its `module` line, its imports, and its declarations, each on lines of its own.

    lazy val sourceFile: Parser[Module] = here ~ (kw("module") ~! (dottedName <~ lineEnd)) ~ rep(kw("import") ~! (dottedName <~ lineEnd) ^^ (match (_, n) -> n)) ~ rep(item <~ lineEnd) ^^ (match (((at, (_, n)), imports), items) -> Module(n, imports, items, at))

## Running it

    // The module `text` holds, or its first syntax error.
    parseModule(text: string) -> ParseResult[Module] = Kite.parseAll(sourceFile, text)

    // One expression, as a statement reads it.
    parseExpr(text: string) -> ParseResult[Expr] = Kite.parseAll(lined.expr, text)

    // One statement.
    parseStmt(text: string) -> ParseResult[Stmt] = Kite.parseAll(stmt, text)

    // One type.
    parseType(text: string) -> ParseResult[TypeRef] = Kite.parseAll(typeRef, text)

    // One pattern.
    parsePattern(text: string) -> ParseResult[Pattern] = Kite.parseAll(pattern, text)
